1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,000
One of the world's most beloved monuments,

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
a magnet for tourists, artists, poets, and lovers.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:11,000
A great symbol of national pride.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
But in the beginning, it was called a factory chimney,

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
useless, monstrous, and mercantile.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
And it was only designed to last 20 years.

7
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, La Tour Eiffel, The Eiffel Tower, on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,000
The world watches hopelessly as Nazi invaders mount a blitzkrieg

9
00:00:33,001 --> 00:00:37,000
attack against France and march triumphantly through the streets

10
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:39,000
of Paris.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
But behind the tears of a defeated people

12
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:10,740
comes the rumbling of rebellion.

13
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:17,380
Enraged French citizens answer General de Gaulle's command

14
00:01:17,480 --> 00:01:19,780
and fight for freedom with an underground organization

15
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:23,860
committed to sabotage and other acts of defiance.

16
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:27,760
This resistance grew bit by bit by bit

17
00:01:27,761 --> 00:01:32,460
This resistance grew bit by bit, comprised of both men and women,

18
00:01:32,780 --> 00:01:36,320
regardless of political, moral, or religious persuasion.

19
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:42,000
I became a founder of the Parisian Liberation Committee

20
00:01:42,001 --> 00:01:45,320
and the organization that directed the resistance

21
00:01:45,321 --> 00:01:47,220
and the national insurrection.

22
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:53,920
Hitler himself visited France during the occupation.

23
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:56,220
He marveled at the great monuments.

24
00:01:56,221 --> 00:01:57,720
He reveled in his conquest.

25
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:04,800
Even the Eiffel Tower, 51 years old and over 1,000 feet tall,

26
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:08,960
became a hostage as Germans exploited its great height,

27
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:13,200
using the tower as an antenna to send coded radio signals

28
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:14,040
to their armies.

29
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:19,040
Ironically, the silhouette of the tower

30
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:21,280
against the Paris skyline offered hope

31
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:24,280
to the free French underground.

32
00:02:24,281 --> 00:02:29,280
It was a symbol of liberty, a symbol of France.

33
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:32,080
Under the occupation, the idea predominated

34
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:35,720
among all the French to know that one day one would be free

35
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:39,120
to climb up the Eiffel Tower.

36
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:42,240
When Allied troops finally liberated Paris,

37
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:44,640
the handful of free Frenchmen celebrated

38
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:47,540
by climbing the Eiffel Tower and unfurling

39
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:50,740
the French tricolor flag.

40
00:02:50,741 --> 00:02:55,240
Down below, citizens spat and cursed at the fleeing Germans.

41
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:56,980
But from the pinnacle of the tower,

42
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:02,780
liberty, equality, and fraternity reclaimed its mantle of glory.

43
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:06,380
For most of the world, the Eiffel Tower is France.

44
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:10,000
It is light and hope, poetry and magic.

45
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:14,500
Her complex geometry holds the dreams of lovers.

46
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:17,500
The French may enjoy the distinct honor of having

47
00:03:17,501 --> 00:03:21,380
of having created and nurtured this international monument.

48
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:24,980
But her home is in the hearts of free people everywhere.

49
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:35,260
There is something about great height

50
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:39,120
that suggests a spirit of triumph to the soul.

51
00:03:39,220 --> 00:03:41,360
It forces people's vision to heaven,

52
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:43,560
while it offers a calming perspective

53
00:03:43,660 --> 00:03:45,360
to the chaos of the world below.

54
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:50,700
Throughout the ages, people have challenged architectural

55
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:55,500
and engineering limits to achieve great structural height,

56
00:03:55,600 --> 00:04:00,040
sometimes to spy on enemies approaching from the distance,

57
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:05,260
and at other times in an attempt to get closer to God.

58
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:08,720
There are those who insist on finding practical applications

59
00:04:08,820 --> 00:04:10,060
for towers.

60
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:12,060
They make good broadcast antennas,

61
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:16,000
or they're a fine place for a revolving restaurant.

62
00:04:16,001 --> 00:04:21,900
But the real appeal of towers comes from a pure notion.

63
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,940
Towers are a celebration of will.

64
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,340
They speak to our ambitions.

65
00:04:28,440 --> 00:04:33,560
They are the joyous declaration that we exist.

66
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:37,760
The world's best love tower is the Eiffel Tower in Paris.

67
00:04:37,860 --> 00:04:43,660
Its soaring height, its delicate lines, its airy volume,

68
00:04:43,661 --> 00:04:47,160
all reflect the best part of the French spirit.

69
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:54,140
Designed and built in 1880, it was the centerpiece

70
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:58,280
in a celebration of the bloody French Revolution 100 years

71
00:04:58,380 --> 00:05:01,240
earlier.

72
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:06,080
On July 14, 1789, in a flood of revolutionary passion,

73
00:05:06,180 --> 00:05:09,820
Parisian artisans led an attack against French aristocracy

74
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:13,120
and stormed the Bastille, the infamous prison

75
00:05:13,121 --> 00:05:16,220
where the poor and politically incorrect were locked away.

76
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:22,960
The insurrection spread across France,

77
00:05:23,060 --> 00:05:27,760
and patriots rallied to the battle cry of freedom.

78
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:31,280
The Marseillaise.

79
00:05:31,380 --> 00:05:33,780
Let us go, children of the fatherland.

80
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:36,080
Our day of glory has arrived.

81
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:38,380
Against us stands tyranny.

82
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:41,220
The bloody flag is raised.

83
00:05:41,221 --> 00:05:45,700
Come together in the countryside to lower these savage soldiers.

84
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:48,460
They come right into our arms to cut the throats

85
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:51,200
of your sons, your country.

86
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:54,640
To arm citizens, form your battalions.

87
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:57,640
Let us march, that their impure blood

88
00:05:57,740 --> 00:05:59,040
should water our fields.

89
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:05,440
This nationalistic eruption reverberated across Europe

90
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:09,320
and set the stage for modern democratic movements.

91
00:06:09,321 --> 00:06:11,820
And the French people were justifiably proud

92
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:17,260
of the positive contributions that their revolution had made.

93
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:19,300
But a century later, the French ego

94
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:23,800
was bruised after a devastating military defeat to Germany.

95
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:26,700
The idea of mounting a spectacular international

96
00:06:26,800 --> 00:06:31,140
exposition seemed like a grand way to remember past glory

97
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:33,880
and forget about contemporary setbacks.

98
00:06:33,980 --> 00:06:37,820
They were still sad because there was,

99
00:06:37,821 --> 00:06:42,220
There was, in 1870, a terrible war with Germany,

100
00:06:42,221 --> 00:06:47,060
and we had lost two provinces, Alsace and Lorraine.

101
00:06:47,061 --> 00:06:49,040
And since for that, the French people

102
00:06:49,041 --> 00:06:52,660
were very pleased to have this exhibition to show

103
00:06:52,661 --> 00:06:56,700
they were very great.

104
00:06:56,701 --> 00:06:59,440
The country was quite rich.

105
00:06:59,441 --> 00:07:02,320
It was decided that there needed to be a centerpiece

106
00:07:02,321 --> 00:07:06,020
to the celebration, an artistic statement which would loom

107
00:07:06,021 --> 00:07:07,560
over the crowds.

108
00:07:07,561 --> 00:07:13,560
The bravado of the industrial age suggested an engineering accomplishment, a tower.

109
00:07:13,561 --> 00:07:21,560
The tower was important as something very clever in technology, you see.

110
00:07:21,561 --> 00:07:28,560
Nobody in the world was able to build a tower of a thousand feet, you see.

111
00:07:28,561 --> 00:07:31,560
Lots of people tried in America.

112
00:07:31,561 --> 00:07:34,860
There were lots of plans of towers,

113
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:37,400
but they never built them.

114
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:40,460
A competition was held for a design.

115
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:44,800
Architects from all over France submitted elaborate plans.

116
00:07:44,900 --> 00:07:50,120
The first one was done by a man called Maurice Coquin.

117
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:56,160
He was one of the first engineers in Gustave Eiffel's staff.

118
00:07:56,260 --> 00:08:00,760
Gustave Eiffel was France's premier architect engineer.

119
00:08:00,761 --> 00:08:04,760
His reputation sprang from his visionary work on bridges.

120
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:07,100
He explored the revolutionary concept

121
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:10,340
of metal frame construction.

122
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:13,800
His genius attracted the finest architects in France

123
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:15,380
to his studio.

124
00:08:15,480 --> 00:08:17,980
And Eiffel's association with the project virtually

125
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:22,280
guaranteed its funding and completion.

126
00:08:22,380 --> 00:08:25,160
Two young engineers on Eiffel's staff,

127
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:27,720
Maurice Coquelin and Emile Nogier,

128
00:08:27,721 --> 00:08:30,760
actually created the concept of the 300-meter tower

129
00:08:30,860 --> 00:08:35,460
for the 1890 exhibition in Paris.

130
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:37,500
They sketched the first crude designs

131
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:40,800
for a framed structure, then called on architect Stephen

132
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:44,700
Salvestre to add ornamental flourishes, the arching

133
00:08:44,800 --> 00:08:49,040
floral elements and sculptural nuances.

134
00:08:49,140 --> 00:08:53,380
Their romantic inspiration was an 1887 French architectural

135
00:08:53,480 --> 00:08:57,340
notion that one could touch the sky from a structure if it

136
00:08:57,341 --> 00:09:00,940
if it reached the impossible height of 1,000 feet.

137
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:04,140
Eiffel saw their plans and shared their dream.

138
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:07,680
Their work was soon adopted by Mr. Eiffel.

139
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:10,780
At first, Eiffel agreed to add his name to the contest

140
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:13,820
submission to improve its chances of winning.

141
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:16,660
But he quickly became interested in the tower concept

142
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:20,500
and began to add his own unique flourishes.

143
00:09:20,600 --> 00:09:22,640
Once Eiffel's name was on a project,

144
00:09:22,740 --> 00:09:26,040
everyone knew the outcome of the competition.

145
00:09:26,041 --> 00:09:28,240
With his social and political connections,

146
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:30,740
Eiffel was well equipped to push the project

147
00:09:30,840 --> 00:09:32,880
through the Parisian bureaucracy.

148
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:35,820
And he had the technical ability to transform the project

149
00:09:35,920 --> 00:09:41,560
from a paper design to a three-dimensional reality.

150
00:09:41,660 --> 00:09:43,960
The tower was designed to represent France's

151
00:09:44,060 --> 00:09:47,560
participation in the Industrial Revolution.

152
00:09:47,660 --> 00:09:50,540
As a symbol and as an engineering challenge,

153
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:53,960
there was a lot riding on the success of the tower.

154
00:09:53,961 --> 00:09:56,660
was determined to exhibit the same innovative spirit

155
00:09:56,760 --> 00:09:59,440
and construction that he had employed in the design.

156
00:10:01,860 --> 00:10:04,940
One design entered in the tower competition for the French

157
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:07,540
Revolution Centennial Exposition was

158
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:09,680
for 1,000-foot-high guillotine.

159
00:10:14,680 --> 00:10:20,880
It is air and light, form and motion, as sturdy as man's

160
00:10:20,881 --> 00:10:25,660
resolve and as ethereal as his thoughts.

161
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:29,560
The Eiffel Tower is as much an idea of structure

162
00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:32,960
as it is an extraordinary physical monument.

163
00:10:33,060 --> 00:10:34,620
And the process of its construction

164
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:37,460
reflects both the concept of prefabrication

165
00:10:37,560 --> 00:10:40,400
from the Industrial Age and the advanced building

166
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:42,600
theories of the dawning 20th century.

167
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:49,700
February 28, 1887, Gustav Eiffel gathered a crowd

168
00:10:49,701 --> 00:10:53,440
of dignitaries to witness the commencement of construction.

169
00:10:53,540 --> 00:10:56,800
He was 53 years old, and this tower

170
00:10:56,900 --> 00:10:58,740
was to be his crowning achievement.

171
00:11:02,140 --> 00:11:05,080
As the ceremonies proceeded, 50 engineers

172
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:09,520
were still drafting over 5,300 detailed blueprints

173
00:11:09,620 --> 00:11:14,160
for the 132 workmen at the site.

174
00:11:14,260 --> 00:11:17,700
It would take four months to lay the foundation for the legs.

175
00:11:17,701 --> 00:11:21,240
Two pillars were set on 6 1⁄2-foot-thick concrete slabs

176
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:23,840
set 23 feet below ground.

177
00:11:23,940 --> 00:11:27,240
The remaining two legs were positioned so close to the Seine

178
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:30,680
River that watertight metal dams were lowered into the damp

179
00:11:30,780 --> 00:11:34,380
earth so the concrete could set beneath the seeping water line.

180
00:11:38,320 --> 00:11:40,220
The square encompassed by the four legs

181
00:11:40,320 --> 00:11:45,460
was 426 feet on each side, broad enough to evenly distribute

182
00:11:45,461 --> 00:11:49,700
distribute the structure 7,000 tons of iron.

183
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:53,060
Onto these foundations arose masonry bases embedded

184
00:11:53,160 --> 00:11:57,200
with two anchoring bolts for each of the four feet.

185
00:11:57,300 --> 00:12:00,540
From here, the legs would rise at a sharp 60 degree angle

186
00:12:00,640 --> 00:12:04,580
as hollow framework beams.

187
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:07,720
The beams were made of angle brackets and flat bars

188
00:12:07,820 --> 00:12:11,880
riveted together with stiffeners attached to the sides.

189
00:12:11,980 --> 00:12:15,460
Four of these assemblies combined to form the entire beam.

190
00:12:15,461 --> 00:12:18,360
The beam had the same structural dynamic as a series

191
00:12:18,460 --> 00:12:19,760
of interlocking cubes.

192
00:12:22,560 --> 00:12:25,900
And this is the genius of Eiffel's design.

193
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,580
The framework support is as sturdy as solid stone at only

194
00:12:29,680 --> 00:12:32,140
a fraction of the weight.

195
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:36,020
It was also easily erected with standardized, inexpensive,

196
00:12:36,120 --> 00:12:37,620
prefabricated materials.

197
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:45,020
Eventually, 18,000 metal parts and 2,500,000 rivets

198
00:12:45,021 --> 00:12:48,560
would comprise the tower.

199
00:12:48,660 --> 00:12:52,220
Yet all of the pieces are either flat bars, angle brackets,

200
00:12:52,320 --> 00:12:53,020
or plates.

201
00:12:56,640 --> 00:12:58,260
The first stage of the construction

202
00:12:58,360 --> 00:13:00,460
was also the most critical.

203
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:03,100
All four legs needed to rise simultaneously

204
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:08,380
and meet on an exact horizontal plane at the tower's first floor.

205
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:11,480
The radical angle demanded scaffolding support for each

206
00:13:11,580 --> 00:13:13,780
of the legs, and the center scaffold

207
00:13:13,781 --> 00:13:16,620
to support the center ring girder, which held the structure

208
00:13:16,720 --> 00:13:18,820
together.

209
00:13:18,920 --> 00:13:22,920
800-ton thrust jacks operated by manual hydraulic pumps

210
00:13:23,020 --> 00:13:25,860
installed under each leg helped to raise and lower the four

211
00:13:25,960 --> 00:13:31,640
sides independently in order to reach the required precision.

212
00:13:31,740 --> 00:13:34,560
When this was achieved, Gustav Eiffel

213
00:13:34,660 --> 00:13:37,400
knew there was nothing that could stop the completion

214
00:13:37,500 --> 00:13:41,840
of his dream, although there were those who would try.

215
00:13:41,841 --> 00:13:45,340
Eiffel was annoyed by people who live near the tower,

216
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:47,780
who said that it would fall on the houses.

217
00:13:47,880 --> 00:13:50,580
And nobody would cover the risk.

218
00:13:50,680 --> 00:13:52,780
He covered it by himself.

219
00:13:52,880 --> 00:13:55,480
Likewise, many people feared that the tower

220
00:13:55,580 --> 00:13:58,080
would destroy the Paris skyline.

221
00:13:58,180 --> 00:14:02,500
When it was started to be constructed,

222
00:14:02,600 --> 00:14:04,900
some people tried to stop it, you see.

223
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:08,400
And some painters, writers, architects

224
00:14:08,500 --> 00:14:11,840
wrote a letter saying it's a scandal.

225
00:14:11,841 --> 00:14:14,580
The Paris arts community saw the tower

226
00:14:14,680 --> 00:14:20,420
as a rude industrial imposition on the city's beauty.

227
00:14:20,520 --> 00:14:22,380
Dozens of French writers and painters

228
00:14:22,480 --> 00:14:25,380
attacked the tower in a public declaration.

229
00:14:25,480 --> 00:14:28,220
I feel Paris is threatened, claimed one artist,

230
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:32,860
by this positively tragic lampstand issuing from her stomach.

231
00:14:36,060 --> 00:14:38,900
Yiffel responded with equal passion.

232
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,440
The tower will be the highest building ever raised

233
00:14:41,441 --> 00:14:46,280
he exclaimed, will it not have a majesty of its own?

234
00:14:56,380 --> 00:15:00,720
By April 1, 1888, the 300-meter tower of Paris

235
00:15:00,820 --> 00:15:04,160
was an eruption of iron lifting skyward

236
00:15:04,260 --> 00:15:07,160
and carrying with it the spirit of France.

237
00:15:07,260 --> 00:15:09,740
And finally, the general public, it seemed,

238
00:15:09,741 --> 00:15:10,740
was falling in love.

239
00:15:13,740 --> 00:15:16,480
The people in Paris, general public,

240
00:15:16,580 --> 00:15:20,220
really felt a very strong feeling of admiration

241
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:21,920
toward this tower.

242
00:15:22,020 --> 00:15:24,760
What had begun as the skeletal suggestion of form

243
00:15:24,860 --> 00:15:28,620
was now exhibiting grand, flowing design,

244
00:15:28,720 --> 00:15:32,600
the graceful rise of the legs, the arcing, decorative additions

245
00:15:32,700 --> 00:15:37,060
contrasting with the stern regimen of exposed bolts.

246
00:15:37,061 --> 00:15:40,160
Here was a work of art that spoke to an aggressive world

247
00:15:40,260 --> 00:15:42,300
preparing for dramatic change.

248
00:15:45,200 --> 00:15:47,500
The work progressed with lightning speed.

249
00:15:47,600 --> 00:15:51,000
Ifel was mounting the operation as if he were conducting a war

250
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:54,320
with crisp military precision.

251
00:15:54,420 --> 00:15:57,820
The structure was, in fact, prefabricated

252
00:15:57,920 --> 00:16:00,220
in the Eiffel factory.

253
00:16:00,320 --> 00:16:03,020
While the tower was assembled, at the most,

254
00:16:03,021 --> 00:16:06,620
At the most, you had something like 300 people working

255
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:08,320
on the site.

256
00:16:08,420 --> 00:16:10,120
Bits of the structure littered the site

257
00:16:10,220 --> 00:16:13,700
like scattered pieces of a child's erector set.

258
00:16:13,800 --> 00:16:16,060
Riveting teams worked in groups of four

259
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:19,040
to keep up with the frantic pace.

260
00:16:19,140 --> 00:16:21,240
As soon as one load of iron was attached,

261
00:16:21,340 --> 00:16:24,240
four 12-ton cranes would deliver more,

262
00:16:24,340 --> 00:16:27,480
crawling up the guide shafts where future elevators would

263
00:16:27,580 --> 00:16:29,340
soar.

264
00:16:29,440 --> 00:16:31,920
The second floor was finished a little over a year

265
00:16:31,921 --> 00:16:33,680
after the start of construction.

266
00:16:33,681 --> 00:16:37,700
And less than a year after that, on March 31, 1889,

267
00:16:37,701 --> 00:16:39,600
the entire tower was complete.

268
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:47,000
It had taken only two years, two months, and five days

269
00:16:47,001 --> 00:16:49,980
to build what would be the world's tallest structure

270
00:16:49,981 --> 00:16:51,940
for the next 40 years.

271
00:16:51,941 --> 00:16:54,740
And remarkably, there was not a single death

272
00:16:54,741 --> 00:16:58,720
as a result of the challenging and dangerous work.

273
00:16:58,721 --> 00:17:02,960
He fell himself was the first to climb the 1,710 steps

274
00:17:02,961 --> 00:17:07,000
to the summit and unfurl the French tricolor flag.

275
00:17:07,001 --> 00:17:10,340
Beneath him was the city of Paris.

276
00:17:10,341 --> 00:17:13,600
It seemed as if its soul was being funneled up the mighty

277
00:17:13,601 --> 00:17:17,080
legs of the tower and sprayed across the sky

278
00:17:17,081 --> 00:17:20,980
in a celestial celebration.

279
00:17:20,981 --> 00:17:23,920
When the 1889 Universal Exposition of Paris

280
00:17:23,921 --> 00:17:26,440
was officially opened, the glittering centerpiece

281
00:17:26,441 --> 00:17:28,580
was the 300-meter tower.

282
00:17:28,581 --> 00:17:32,020
Officials called it the greatest crowd flabbergasting machine

283
00:17:32,021 --> 00:17:33,620
ever built.

284
00:17:33,621 --> 00:17:37,320
But soon, it was simply known as the Eiffel Tower.

285
00:17:42,020 --> 00:17:46,240
The final height of the Eiffel Tower was 990 feet,

286
00:17:46,241 --> 00:17:49,760
or slightly more in hot weather when it expands

287
00:17:49,761 --> 00:17:51,300
by another seven inches.

288
00:17:55,240 --> 00:17:57,820
The tower was called at first the Tower

289
00:17:57,821 --> 00:18:01,180
of 320 meters, even in the beginning,

290
00:18:01,181 --> 00:18:07,180
the pylon of 320 meters, then the 320 meter tower.

291
00:18:07,181 --> 00:18:10,180
I don't know exactly when it became the Tower of Mr.

292
00:18:10,181 --> 00:18:12,260
Eiffel and later the Eiffel Tower.

293
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:19,100
I think it escaped him that it would go any further

294
00:18:19,101 --> 00:18:22,940
than a very high functional tower of great dimension.

295
00:18:22,941 --> 00:18:32,940
The Eiffel Tower is rooted in both the majestic and the mundane.

296
00:18:32,941 --> 00:18:35,980
It may have been the tallest structure on the planet,

297
00:18:35,981 --> 00:18:38,580
but it was equally cherished by Mr. Eiffel

298
00:18:38,581 --> 00:18:42,260
because of the tremendous personal income it generated.

299
00:18:42,261 --> 00:18:46,580
You know, Mr. Eiffel practically

300
00:18:46,581 --> 00:18:48,940
funded the construction of the tower.

301
00:18:48,941 --> 00:18:51,920
In fact, he paid for most of it, and he made a deal.

302
00:18:51,921 --> 00:18:54,960
He said, if you give me the right to work on it for 20 years,

303
00:18:55,060 --> 00:18:56,860
I'll agree to finance it 80%.

304
00:18:56,861 --> 00:18:59,680
If he had not believed in the success of his tower,

305
00:19:00,100 --> 00:19:01,700
he would not have jumped into this venture

306
00:19:01,701 --> 00:19:03,860
in which he succeeded so magnificently.

307
00:19:07,220 --> 00:19:09,660
Eiffel had a private apartment on the tower

308
00:19:09,661 --> 00:19:12,180
from which he conducted scientific experiments

309
00:19:12,181 --> 00:19:14,440
and met with important dignitaries.

310
00:19:15,320 --> 00:19:17,680
All the while, he could watch the elevators

311
00:19:17,681 --> 00:19:21,440
lifting nearly 2 million tourists to the top in the first year.

312
00:19:21,920 --> 00:19:25,860
representing over $1 million income to Eiffel,

313
00:19:25,861 --> 00:19:29,960
as he personally garnered the receipts from ticket sales.

314
00:19:44,780 --> 00:19:47,080
Contrasting opinions of Mr. Eiffel

315
00:19:47,081 --> 00:19:50,180
spring from the multifaceted and conflicting nature

316
00:19:50,181 --> 00:19:52,940
of his character.

317
00:19:52,941 --> 00:19:55,580
He may not have been an artist, but his approach

318
00:19:55,581 --> 00:19:57,680
to metal frame construction established

319
00:19:57,681 --> 00:20:01,020
a structural aesthetic which is still admired today.

320
00:20:04,160 --> 00:20:07,160
He may have been a national hero after building the tower

321
00:20:07,161 --> 00:20:10,660
in 1889, but the next year he was convicted

322
00:20:10,661 --> 00:20:13,180
of profiteering in the failed French effort

323
00:20:13,181 --> 00:20:16,740
to construct a Panama Canal.

324
00:20:16,741 --> 00:20:19,740
The canal was the dream of French engineer Ferdinand

325
00:20:19,741 --> 00:20:22,040
who had triumphed with his Suez Canal

326
00:20:22,140 --> 00:20:26,180
and was determined to duplicate the feat in Panama.

327
00:20:26,280 --> 00:20:29,880
De Lesseps invited Eiffel to suggest an engineering approach

328
00:20:29,980 --> 00:20:32,120
to accomplish the jungle waterway,

329
00:20:32,220 --> 00:20:36,260
and Eiffel proposed an innovative system of locks.

330
00:20:36,360 --> 00:20:38,460
That idea was rejected by De Lesseps,

331
00:20:38,560 --> 00:20:42,200
and the result was disastrous.

332
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:45,400
Canals cannot be carved through swamps and jungle.

333
00:20:45,500 --> 00:20:48,360
The attempt proved so expensive and unproductive,

334
00:20:48,361 --> 00:20:52,360
The French government was nearly thrown into bankruptcy.

335
00:20:52,460 --> 00:20:54,500
The political fallout was severe.

336
00:20:54,600 --> 00:20:57,600
Anyone even remotely associated with the failed effort

337
00:20:57,700 --> 00:21:02,740
was vilified, and Eiffel was sentenced to 24 months in prison.

338
00:21:02,840 --> 00:21:04,680
That judgment was later overturned,

339
00:21:04,780 --> 00:21:06,840
but by then, Eiffel had lost his appetite

340
00:21:06,940 --> 00:21:09,340
for the construction business.

341
00:21:09,440 --> 00:21:14,380
Eiffel was considered by all engineers like a god, you see.

342
00:21:14,381 --> 00:21:18,760
And he was very, he was also a man of science.

343
00:21:18,860 --> 00:21:19,780
He loved science.

344
00:21:20,320 --> 00:21:24,880
And when, after the tower, he stopped building things.

345
00:21:25,520 --> 00:21:29,200
Instead, Eiffel, now 73 years old,

346
00:21:29,640 --> 00:21:32,220
dedicated himself to pure scientific study,

347
00:21:32,740 --> 00:21:35,660
specifically the exploration of aerodynamics.

348
00:21:37,060 --> 00:21:40,360
Other French visionaries, like author Jules Verne,

349
00:21:40,361 --> 00:21:42,940
had applied crude 19th century notions

350
00:21:42,941 --> 00:21:46,940
to the concept of flight, as illustrated in this early film,

351
00:21:47,040 --> 00:21:49,880
proposing a trip to the moon.

352
00:21:49,980 --> 00:21:52,980
Yiffel realized that advancement in aviation

353
00:21:53,080 --> 00:21:55,220
would need to be based on the scientific study

354
00:21:55,320 --> 00:21:57,260
of the natural elements.

355
00:21:57,360 --> 00:21:59,460
But while some well-meaning researchers

356
00:21:59,560 --> 00:22:02,160
were mired in the idea of outlandish mechanical

357
00:22:02,260 --> 00:22:05,900
contraptions, and others were attaching feathered wings

358
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,700
to their arms and tossing themselves off the tower

359
00:22:08,800 --> 00:22:12,500
like birds, Yiffel returned to his analytical roots

360
00:22:12,501 --> 00:22:15,640
and built a workshop that still operates today.

361
00:22:19,340 --> 00:22:23,400
Mr. Eiffel, well, he made the first wind resistance test

362
00:22:23,401 --> 00:22:26,960
because all of his life he had problems with the wind.

363
00:22:27,880 --> 00:22:29,780
That was his enemy, number one.

364
00:22:30,580 --> 00:22:36,240
And in 1906 to 1909, he began to question aerodynamic resistance,

365
00:22:36,520 --> 00:22:39,040
and that's why he made this first wind tunnel.

366
00:22:39,660 --> 00:22:41,540
One can say that in this wind tunnel,

367
00:22:41,541 --> 00:22:43,540
where you're standing right now,

368
00:22:43,840 --> 00:22:46,060
French aviationary studies began.

369
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:54,780
I must specify that the tunnel, the ventilator, the motor,

370
00:22:54,980 --> 00:22:56,540
and all of these are originals.

371
00:22:56,740 --> 00:22:57,440
We don't touch them.

372
00:22:57,960 --> 00:22:59,700
We leave them in their original state.

373
00:23:00,920 --> 00:23:03,140
As for measuring devices, of course,

374
00:23:03,141 --> 00:23:05,200
we adapted them for modern functions.

375
00:23:05,201 --> 00:23:12,400
Mr. Eiffel was an extraordinary engineer.

376
00:23:12,940 --> 00:23:16,780
We've examined all he did during his lifetime and all that he envisioned.

377
00:23:17,560 --> 00:23:22,600
And today, we are doing things that Mr. Eiffel already did in 1917.

378
00:23:25,420 --> 00:23:32,040
Eiffel's biggest vision, the 300-meter tower, was only supposed to stand for 20 years.

379
00:23:32,041 --> 00:23:35,840
But Eiffel had grown accustomed to using the structure

380
00:23:35,940 --> 00:23:39,140
as his personal reception area, a way

381
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:45,180
to maintain his high profile over Parisian society.

382
00:23:45,280 --> 00:23:48,120
Eiffel launched an aggressive campaign

383
00:23:48,220 --> 00:23:52,600
to keep his tower from the wrecking ball.

384
00:23:52,700 --> 00:23:56,200
He conducted over 5,000 experiments in his lofty

385
00:23:56,300 --> 00:24:02,040
laboratory, but it was radio that saved the day.

386
00:24:02,041 --> 00:24:04,200
and the tower.

387
00:24:04,201 --> 00:24:07,740
The giant metal structure was the perfect antenna.

388
00:24:07,741 --> 00:24:10,800
And from the top, signals could be transmitted as far away

389
00:24:10,801 --> 00:24:13,040
as North America.

390
00:24:13,041 --> 00:24:16,100
Although destined for demolition in 1909,

391
00:24:16,101 --> 00:24:21,160
Eiffel's tower won a reprieve as radio helped France monitor

392
00:24:21,161 --> 00:24:26,580
the dark clouds of war looming over Germany.

393
00:24:26,581 --> 00:24:30,540
In 1914, World War swept over the European continent.

394
00:24:30,541 --> 00:24:32,940
For Paris, the conflict came so close

395
00:24:33,040 --> 00:24:38,080
that soldiers often took taxis to travel to the front lines.

396
00:24:38,180 --> 00:24:40,080
But with the help of the Eiffel Tower,

397
00:24:40,180 --> 00:24:44,180
generals could listen in on the movements of the enemy

398
00:24:44,280 --> 00:24:47,700
and declare zeppelin alerts from their elevated perch.

399
00:24:50,800 --> 00:24:56,140
In 1923, five years after the last echoes of war,

400
00:24:56,240 --> 00:24:59,040
Gustav Eiffel died.

401
00:24:59,041 --> 00:25:02,480
Throughout his 91 years, his vision propelled France

402
00:25:02,580 --> 00:25:05,720
to the forefront of emerging technologies,

403
00:25:05,820 --> 00:25:08,420
and his system of simple interlocking elements

404
00:25:08,520 --> 00:25:13,320
anticipated the basic principles of 20th century architecture.

405
00:25:13,420 --> 00:25:16,400
Five months after it opened, the Eiffel Tower

406
00:25:16,500 --> 00:25:19,200
had earned back 75% of its cost.

407
00:25:23,260 --> 00:25:25,560
After the devastation of a world war,

408
00:25:25,660 --> 00:25:28,500
Paris was anxious to lift its battered spirits

409
00:25:28,501 --> 00:25:31,360
embrace a brighter day.

410
00:25:31,460 --> 00:25:34,340
Yet some sensed a subtle, desperate undercurrent

411
00:25:34,440 --> 00:25:36,980
to the celebratory esprit.

412
00:25:37,080 --> 00:25:38,880
It was as if the frantic good times

413
00:25:38,980 --> 00:25:42,380
were masking the many issues that the war had left unsettled.

414
00:25:46,220 --> 00:25:50,360
Paris in the 20s attracted a lost generation,

415
00:25:50,460 --> 00:25:55,060
disillusioned with traditional values and philosophies.

416
00:25:55,160 --> 00:25:57,400
Some of these post-war Parisians were actually

417
00:25:57,401 --> 00:26:00,440
artists and intellectuals searching for meaning

418
00:26:00,540 --> 00:26:02,500
in a seemingly chaotic world.

419
00:26:05,240 --> 00:26:10,840
F. Scott Fitzgerald and his wife Zelda, Charlie Chaplin,

420
00:26:10,940 --> 00:26:13,780
movie stars, painters, and a mixed assortment

421
00:26:13,880 --> 00:26:17,760
of creative individuals became attracted to a French revolution

422
00:26:17,860 --> 00:26:21,960
in thinking called existentialism.

423
00:26:22,060 --> 00:26:25,860
Is what we are the sum of our existence, our time and poetry

424
00:26:25,861 --> 00:26:30,260
in poetry nothing more than absurdist fantasy.

425
00:26:30,261 --> 00:26:34,440
Darker themes of alienation started to emerge in the arts.

426
00:26:34,441 --> 00:26:37,900
And ironically, the tower provided a visual and symbolic link

427
00:26:37,901 --> 00:26:42,000
to a lighter and more stable time.

428
00:26:42,001 --> 00:26:44,660
After all, Eiffel had created weightless beauty

429
00:26:44,661 --> 00:26:48,220
from dense, heavy metal.

430
00:26:48,221 --> 00:26:50,420
Very reassuring concept.

431
00:26:50,421 --> 00:26:55,420
The Eiffel Tower is not only the iron composing it,

432
00:26:55,520 --> 00:27:00,360
but also what's inside, air, captured in a latticework

433
00:27:00,460 --> 00:27:01,860
of metal.

434
00:27:01,960 --> 00:27:06,440
Some visitors feel that they are in the middle of nowhere.

435
00:27:06,540 --> 00:27:09,700
The sensation suggests that it's not only the structure,

436
00:27:09,800 --> 00:27:13,780
but also its void, which is the essential material

437
00:27:13,880 --> 00:27:14,840
of the Eiffel Tower.

438
00:27:17,340 --> 00:27:19,520
The tower's bold composition seemed

439
00:27:19,521 --> 00:27:26,520
seemed a poetic link between the 19th and 20th centuries,

440
00:27:26,620 --> 00:27:31,140
a link which would be brutally severed when fascism, racism,

441
00:27:31,240 --> 00:27:34,760
and systematic murder erupted from the ashes of Germany's

442
00:27:34,860 --> 00:27:36,800
defeat in the First World War.

443
00:27:43,540 --> 00:27:48,240
May 1940, in the terrifying panic of a Nazi blitzkrieg,

444
00:27:48,241 --> 00:27:51,240
Paris and the Eiffel Tower were taken hostage.

445
00:27:53,940 --> 00:27:55,780
But German troops found it difficult

446
00:27:55,880 --> 00:27:59,180
to fully utilize the monument.

447
00:27:59,280 --> 00:28:02,080
Frenchmen had damaged the elevators.

448
00:28:02,180 --> 00:28:07,600
This sabotage kept even Hitler from savoring his conquest.

449
00:28:07,700 --> 00:28:11,200
There's a very famous photo.

450
00:28:11,300 --> 00:28:12,400
He's on the trocadero.

451
00:28:12,500 --> 00:28:14,740
He looks at the tower, but he hasn't been on it.

452
00:28:14,840 --> 00:28:15,840
There were no lifts.

453
00:28:15,940 --> 00:28:18,240
Couldn't go.

454
00:28:18,241 --> 00:28:23,240
Seeing the tower occupied by Nazis enraged French citizens.

455
00:28:23,340 --> 00:28:27,140
Parisians joined together in feudal counterattacks.

456
00:28:27,240 --> 00:28:29,320
The Free French secretly conspired

457
00:28:29,420 --> 00:28:32,020
in the ancient Roman catacombs, which

458
00:28:32,120 --> 00:28:34,480
weave beneath the streets of Paris.

459
00:28:34,580 --> 00:28:38,020
Tunnels hiding the bones of forgotten and vanquished

460
00:28:38,120 --> 00:28:40,120
Frenchmen throughout the centuries

461
00:28:40,220 --> 00:28:44,440
were now also home to the resistance.

462
00:28:44,540 --> 00:28:46,460
There are subterranean pests that

463
00:28:46,461 --> 00:28:48,520
that go underneath the French capital.

464
00:28:49,360 --> 00:28:52,700
The catacombs played an important role in the resistance.

465
00:28:53,300 --> 00:28:57,500
It was a means for us to travel across Paris without being seen

466
00:28:57,501 --> 00:29:01,400
in order to be able to lead the Parisian insurrection on the offense.

467
00:29:04,880 --> 00:29:09,720
We derailed trains because those trains that went to the front lines of the Atlantic

468
00:29:09,721 --> 00:29:11,580
all went behind Paris.

469
00:29:11,581 --> 00:29:18,160
I participated in the sabotage of railroad tracks,

470
00:29:18,820 --> 00:29:22,440
tracks that were obviously meant for trains that transported soldiers.

471
00:29:25,280 --> 00:29:29,640
But one day, we found out about a large train that circled Paris.

472
00:29:32,160 --> 00:29:35,100
We made arrangements, we derailed the train,

473
00:29:35,101 --> 00:29:39,940
and it was just awful because the train was packed with sauerkraut.

474
00:29:39,941 --> 00:29:46,940
There were particles of sauerkraut for miles around.

475
00:29:47,040 --> 00:29:49,580
Hitler despised the Free French

476
00:29:49,680 --> 00:29:53,180
and planned to destroy their most treasured monument

477
00:29:53,280 --> 00:29:54,780
out of spite.

478
00:29:54,880 --> 00:29:56,760
At the war's end, Hitler gave orders

479
00:29:56,860 --> 00:29:58,960
to have the tower bombed.

480
00:29:59,060 --> 00:30:01,420
But General von Cholitz, who had fallen in love

481
00:30:01,520 --> 00:30:04,560
with the city of Paris, gave Ifel's tower

482
00:30:04,660 --> 00:30:06,660
an unauthorized reprieve.

483
00:30:09,940 --> 00:30:20,180
People of Western Europe, a landing was made this morning on the coast of France by troops

484
00:30:20,181 --> 00:30:23,400
of the Allied Expeditionary Force.

485
00:30:23,401 --> 00:30:31,400
The hour of your liberation is approaching.

486
00:30:31,401 --> 00:30:38,700
American tanks finally drove the Nazis from the Eiffel Tower.

487
00:30:38,701 --> 00:30:41,500
But when liberation finally came to Paris,

488
00:30:41,600 --> 00:30:43,940
the French government was in a shambles.

489
00:30:44,040 --> 00:30:46,000
The old leaders were gone.

490
00:30:46,100 --> 00:30:47,940
The next generation was ill-equipped

491
00:30:48,040 --> 00:30:52,240
to raise the country to its former glory.

492
00:30:52,340 --> 00:30:55,320
From 1945 through 1960, the people of France

493
00:30:55,420 --> 00:30:59,620
voted 20 different provisional governments into power.

494
00:30:59,720 --> 00:31:03,320
It was political chaos until General Charles de Gaulle

495
00:31:03,420 --> 00:31:05,660
was finally persuaded to lead the country

496
00:31:05,661 --> 00:31:09,260
of post-war calamity.

497
00:31:09,360 --> 00:31:12,060
Only a man of his strength and broad appeal

498
00:31:12,160 --> 00:31:15,400
could have saved the Republic.

499
00:31:15,500 --> 00:31:17,680
De Gaulle rallied the disheartened country

500
00:31:17,780 --> 00:31:20,400
and gave France back its spirit.

501
00:31:20,500 --> 00:31:23,040
Its soul, however, had always been protected

502
00:31:23,140 --> 00:31:28,080
in the giant gilded cage of the Eiffel Tower.

503
00:31:28,180 --> 00:31:31,120
But just as France needed a new vision for the second half

504
00:31:31,220 --> 00:31:33,680
of the 20th century, the Eiffel Tower

505
00:31:33,681 --> 00:31:36,380
needed a new commitment to its care and maintenance,

506
00:31:36,480 --> 00:31:37,980
if it were to stand at all.

507
00:31:43,760 --> 00:31:45,760
It was designed as the centerpiece

508
00:31:45,860 --> 00:31:49,760
of an international exposition in 1889.

509
00:31:49,860 --> 00:31:53,260
But today, more than a century later, the celebration

510
00:31:53,360 --> 00:31:56,940
continues as well over 1,000 people visit the Eiffel Tower

511
00:31:57,040 --> 00:31:57,900
every hour.

512
00:32:03,680 --> 00:32:10,920
There are four elevators to carry them to the top, one

513
00:32:11,020 --> 00:32:16,920
in each of the tower legs rising at a steep 60-degree angle.

514
00:32:17,020 --> 00:32:21,060
The heart of the hoisting is actually hidden beneath the legs

515
00:32:21,160 --> 00:32:24,060
in a century-old catacomb.

516
00:32:28,000 --> 00:32:32,400
Here, complex contraptions rival Jules Verne's most

517
00:32:32,401 --> 00:32:35,540
Verne's most elaborate fantasies.

518
00:32:35,640 --> 00:32:39,240
The original principle was to have water under pressure

519
00:32:39,340 --> 00:32:42,840
pushing a piston that was pushing a dolly.

520
00:32:42,940 --> 00:32:46,340
The dolly was pulling the cable to heave the car up.

521
00:32:49,160 --> 00:32:53,880
The 100-year-old technology forms an animated mosaic,

522
00:32:53,980 --> 00:32:56,120
giving the impression that the lifting is done

523
00:32:56,220 --> 00:32:59,820
by a giant mechanical toy.

524
00:32:59,821 --> 00:33:05,500
This piston rings which are inside here, there's square cotton wreath about that big,

525
00:33:05,501 --> 00:33:08,820
which you press in, and they're greased with mutton fat.

526
00:33:08,821 --> 00:33:09,820
See what?

527
00:33:09,821 --> 00:33:10,820
See?

528
00:33:10,821 --> 00:33:12,820
Very white, very nice one.

529
00:33:12,821 --> 00:33:19,820
Twice a week here, it smells like having a Mechui, you know, this North African roasted lamb.

530
00:33:19,821 --> 00:33:21,820
That's it.

531
00:33:21,821 --> 00:33:24,820
All technology works beautifully.

532
00:33:26,820 --> 00:33:28,820
That's leather.

533
00:33:29,820 --> 00:33:32,720
That's leather, same material.

534
00:33:32,820 --> 00:33:36,220
But it's compressed, it's shaped by various shapes.

535
00:33:36,320 --> 00:33:40,000
It's true leather, and it's impregnated with oil,

536
00:33:40,100 --> 00:33:41,940
which is made from the hooves of oxen.

537
00:33:47,240 --> 00:33:50,700
In 1980, officials decided to upgrade the system

538
00:33:50,800 --> 00:33:53,880
by blending the old technology with the latest

539
00:33:53,980 --> 00:33:56,580
high-tech equipment.

540
00:33:56,680 --> 00:33:58,540
It's like mixing the engine of a Ferrari

541
00:33:58,541 --> 00:34:01,440
and the engine of a Mack truck.

542
00:34:01,540 --> 00:34:04,240
At the third level, a second bank of elevators

543
00:34:04,340 --> 00:34:08,380
carry passengers vertically to the summit.

544
00:34:08,480 --> 00:34:11,820
Atop the structure, maintenance is nonstop.

545
00:34:11,920 --> 00:34:15,500
Every day, a small but determined team of 25 workers

546
00:34:15,600 --> 00:34:19,420
walk the narrow iron supports for the 18-month job

547
00:34:19,520 --> 00:34:21,900
of painting the tower.

548
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:24,300
The process is the same today as when the tower

549
00:34:24,400 --> 00:34:26,300
was originally painted.

550
00:34:26,301 --> 00:34:30,300
Over 50 tons of paint is required to complete the task,

551
00:34:30,301 --> 00:34:34,300
a task that's repeated every seven years.

552
00:34:34,301 --> 00:34:39,300
I was 18 years old when I began this job in the air

553
00:34:39,301 --> 00:34:41,300
as an industrial painter.

554
00:34:41,301 --> 00:34:47,300
To work on the Eiffel Tower, such an important monument,

555
00:34:47,301 --> 00:34:50,300
I feel privileged and happy.

556
00:34:50,301 --> 00:34:55,300
But painters aren't alone on the elevator.

557
00:34:55,301 --> 00:34:58,060
alone on the elevated web of iron.

558
00:34:58,061 --> 00:35:02,180
Electricians also comb the tower.

559
00:35:02,181 --> 00:35:05,380
Personally, I'd much rather work up here than in an office.

560
00:35:05,381 --> 00:35:06,820
I think my friend agrees.

561
00:35:06,821 --> 00:35:08,980
Yeah, absolutely.

562
00:35:08,981 --> 00:35:10,840
I mean, we get to be outdoors.

563
00:35:10,841 --> 00:35:12,760
We like what we do.

564
00:35:12,761 --> 00:35:15,220
To be able to work on a monument like the Eiffel Tower

565
00:35:15,221 --> 00:35:17,320
is a privilege, I'd say.

566
00:35:19,380 --> 00:35:22,280
The exposed nature of the structure occasionally

567
00:35:22,281 --> 00:35:25,040
allows the elements to inflict damage.

568
00:35:25,041 --> 00:35:27,880
Water pipes can freeze and burst if not protected

569
00:35:27,881 --> 00:35:29,420
by the electrical system.

570
00:35:29,421 --> 00:35:33,660
There are heating bands with thermostats.

571
00:35:33,661 --> 00:35:36,320
As soon as the temperature falls below 40 degrees,

572
00:35:36,321 --> 00:35:38,900
the heating bands kick in.

573
00:35:38,901 --> 00:35:40,500
The piping is heated.

574
00:35:42,640 --> 00:35:44,340
But the main job of the electricians

575
00:35:44,341 --> 00:35:47,340
is to maintain the illuminated beauty.

576
00:35:47,341 --> 00:35:51,580
360 light bulbs are constantly inspected and changed.

577
00:35:55,980 --> 00:36:00,480
Early evening, the sun casts its last golden pink rays

578
00:36:00,481 --> 00:36:04,420
over the city of Paris, the homes, the museums,

579
00:36:04,421 --> 00:36:08,300
the monuments and cathedrals, and the couples who

580
00:36:08,301 --> 00:36:11,660
gather on the tower.

581
00:36:11,661 --> 00:36:15,500
The tower responds as if its own glorious wash of color

582
00:36:15,501 --> 00:36:18,900
is the reflected glow of lovers.

583
00:36:18,901 --> 00:36:20,640
The lighting design is calculated

584
00:36:20,641 --> 00:36:22,680
to enhance the romantic ambience.

585
00:36:28,020 --> 00:36:34,120
The paint on the Eiffel Tower contains a yellow pigment.

586
00:36:34,121 --> 00:36:38,920
Lighting it with yellow light brings out the yellow pigment.

587
00:36:38,921 --> 00:36:42,220
That's been the major source of success in this operation.

588
00:36:45,500 --> 00:36:49,780
The approach is entirely different

589
00:36:49,781 --> 00:36:54,240
because night lighting must interpret the feeling,

590
00:36:54,241 --> 00:36:56,180
even the expression of the monument.

591
00:37:01,820 --> 00:37:06,260
The Eiffel Tower lighting seeks to validate the architecture

592
00:37:06,261 --> 00:37:10,720
of this star attraction in Paris.

593
00:37:10,721 --> 00:37:12,860
It's the star of Paris at night.

594
00:37:15,500 --> 00:37:22,500
The Eiffel Tower has been repainted 17 times.

595
00:37:22,600 --> 00:37:27,040
It has changed color from brown to yellow,

596
00:37:27,140 --> 00:37:32,220
to chestnut brown, and finally to its current color, bronze.

597
00:37:36,320 --> 00:37:38,260
There is a sense of ownership which

598
00:37:38,360 --> 00:37:41,720
is shared among those who work the tower, especially

599
00:37:41,820 --> 00:37:45,500
the employees whose task is to greet visitors.

600
00:37:45,501 --> 00:37:52,500
My job in fact is to organize the tour guides. I am responsible for the group.

601
00:37:52,501 --> 00:37:54,457
We're now in the north pillar.

602
00:37:54,458 --> 00:38:01,500
Each of the four corners of the Eiffel Tower is represented by pillars in which you will find the elevators.

603
00:38:01,501 --> 00:38:08,500
Down there is the beautiful France restaurant and above it the Parisian restaurant, the two restaurants of the first level.

604
00:38:08,501 --> 00:38:13,500
And over there is the Cinemax Gallery,

605
00:38:13,600 --> 00:38:17,500
where you'll find a post office, souvenir shops, buffets,

606
00:38:17,600 --> 00:38:18,640
ice cream vendors.

607
00:38:18,740 --> 00:38:22,740
There are lots of interesting things in the gallery.

608
00:38:22,840 --> 00:38:26,760
The tower was originally designed as a tourist mecca,

609
00:38:26,860 --> 00:38:30,260
and it still serves that lighthearted function today.

610
00:38:30,360 --> 00:38:34,400
Yet its magnificent vistas and staggering height

611
00:38:34,500 --> 00:38:38,500
sometimes attracts a darker element.

612
00:38:38,501 --> 00:38:43,500
People periodically try to jump off the Eiffel Tower.

613
00:38:43,501 --> 00:38:47,500
Already 380 people have committed suicide.

614
00:38:47,501 --> 00:38:50,500
That's an average of about three or four a year.

615
00:38:50,501 --> 00:38:53,500
So we regularly try to control the situation.

616
00:38:57,500 --> 00:38:59,500
We try to reason with them.

617
00:38:59,501 --> 00:39:01,500
We try to find out their motive.

618
00:39:01,501 --> 00:39:03,500
We discuss it with them.

619
00:39:03,501 --> 00:39:05,500
We try to talk with them as much as possible

620
00:39:05,501 --> 00:39:08,100
until security comes, and from then on,

621
00:39:08,101 --> 00:39:10,500
it's the fireman's responsibility.

622
00:39:13,300 --> 00:39:16,040
More often, a tour guide has the opportunity

623
00:39:16,041 --> 00:39:19,440
to see people in a more festive and romantic mood.

624
00:39:22,180 --> 00:39:25,660
I have seen a lot of couples walking arm in arm.

625
00:39:25,661 --> 00:39:28,760
They often come to celebrate an event or an anniversary.

626
00:39:28,761 --> 00:39:39,760
Our job also includes discouraging these people from riding on the Eiffel Tower.

627
00:39:39,761 --> 00:39:44,160
Most of the time we chase them and we try to catch them, and sometimes it's a little

628
00:39:44,161 --> 00:39:47,360
game between the tourists and ourselves.

629
00:39:47,361 --> 00:39:54,520
We are on the third floor of the Eiffel Tower in front of the Jules Verne restaurant.

630
00:39:54,521 --> 00:40:00,520
This restaurant is one of the symbols of Paris precisely because it's located way up on the third floor.

631
00:40:00,521 --> 00:40:04,520
There's only one Eiffel Tower, only one third floor.

632
00:40:04,521 --> 00:40:09,520
It's certainly the best location in the world unless there's a restaurant on top of Mount Everest.

633
00:40:10,520 --> 00:40:19,520
The ceremony and celebration of haute cuisine is a tradition on the tower extending back to its earliest days.

634
00:40:19,521 --> 00:40:22,860
And food is more than nourishment for the French.

635
00:40:22,960 --> 00:40:24,260
It is a passion.

636
00:40:25,560 --> 00:40:28,260
It happened about six months after I arrived in Paris.

637
00:40:28,360 --> 00:40:30,860
I informed my wife that I had a mistress.

638
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:32,460
She just stared at me.

639
00:40:32,560 --> 00:40:34,360
And I said, she's an old mistress.

640
00:40:34,460 --> 00:40:36,040
She looked even more surprised.

641
00:40:36,140 --> 00:40:38,180
After about five minutes, I told her my mistress

642
00:40:38,280 --> 00:40:39,180
is the Eiffel Tower.

643
00:40:42,680 --> 00:40:44,280
Some artists paint.

644
00:40:44,380 --> 00:40:45,780
Others work in stone.

645
00:40:45,880 --> 00:40:49,520
But master chef Alain Rex prefers pâté.

646
00:40:49,521 --> 00:40:52,960
practicing his culinary magic at the Jules Verne,

647
00:40:53,060 --> 00:40:55,120
an exclusive four-star restaurant

648
00:40:55,220 --> 00:40:57,460
midway up the lofty height of the tower.

649
00:41:00,460 --> 00:41:02,960
The view of Paris really inspires me.

650
00:41:03,060 --> 00:41:05,760
It inspires me to create new dishes every day.

651
00:41:10,280 --> 00:41:13,980
We have 115 people employed in the Jules Verne restaurant

652
00:41:14,080 --> 00:41:15,280
alone.

653
00:41:15,380 --> 00:41:18,420
There's a lot of hustle and bustle, a lot of people.

654
00:41:18,421 --> 00:41:20,220
I love that.

655
00:41:20,320 --> 00:41:23,020
Armed with spatulas, whisks, paring knives,

656
00:41:23,120 --> 00:41:25,760
and souffle cups, Chef Rex and his men

657
00:41:25,860 --> 00:41:27,860
fight for the honor of France.

658
00:41:33,860 --> 00:41:37,780
Today's special, a lobster salad and coral sauce.

659
00:41:37,880 --> 00:41:38,780
It's French lobster.

660
00:41:41,880 --> 00:41:45,180
The tower was designed to inspire the common person

661
00:41:45,280 --> 00:41:46,680
to new visions.

662
00:41:46,681 --> 00:41:51,680
It also encourages the imaginations of artists to soar.

663
00:41:51,780 --> 00:41:57,320
At the Jules Verne restaurant, these two dynamics take flight.

664
00:41:57,420 --> 00:42:00,220
It is an appetizing blend that could only happen

665
00:42:00,320 --> 00:42:02,020
in the city of light.

666
00:42:02,021 --> 00:42:03,520
Light.

667
00:42:16,460 --> 00:42:17,560
The city of Paris.

668
00:42:21,140 --> 00:42:26,920
The city of Paris is often called the city of light.

669
00:42:26,921 --> 00:42:31,620
But to what quality of light does that title refer?

670
00:42:31,621 --> 00:42:35,120
Does it suggest the lightness of the French spirit,

671
00:42:35,121 --> 00:42:38,000
the enlightenment of the French culture,

672
00:42:38,001 --> 00:42:41,200
or perhaps the brilliant light of the French sky,

673
00:42:41,201 --> 00:42:44,900
bleaching marble facades by day and by night,

674
00:42:44,901 --> 00:42:47,940
cradling lovers in its rose-colored evening mist?

675
00:42:51,240 --> 00:42:53,680
Maybe it's the magic light of a cabaret

676
00:42:53,681 --> 00:42:55,840
where Toulouse-Lautrec painted and played.

677
00:43:01,620 --> 00:43:06,120
or the glittering sparkle of a merry-go-round reflected

678
00:43:06,220 --> 00:43:09,620
in the eyes of the young at heart.

679
00:43:09,720 --> 00:43:14,260
The light of Paris is all these images and experiences,

680
00:43:14,360 --> 00:43:16,660
yet it finds its most vibrant voice

681
00:43:16,760 --> 00:43:21,580
in the silent exultation of Eiffel's tower.

682
00:43:21,680 --> 00:43:25,380
I believe that the Eiffel Tower has taken

683
00:43:25,480 --> 00:43:28,180
an entirely unique place in the world.

684
00:43:28,280 --> 00:43:30,520
It's almost become a myth.

685
00:43:30,521 --> 00:43:34,520
It sort of glorifies science.

686
00:43:34,620 --> 00:43:38,120
It was built during an era when it was thought that science

687
00:43:38,220 --> 00:43:42,460
and technology would make mankind happy.

688
00:43:42,560 --> 00:43:44,960
I believe also because of its shape,

689
00:43:45,060 --> 00:43:49,980
it is very, very special and can be easily drawn.

690
00:43:50,080 --> 00:43:53,880
I think also that it is a useless object

691
00:43:53,980 --> 00:43:59,020
and that a useless object has a great therapeutic value.

692
00:43:59,021 --> 00:44:02,820
So the Eiffel Tower stands as a monument to those things

693
00:44:02,920 --> 00:44:08,860
we cannot grasp, our joys, our hopes.

694
00:44:08,960 --> 00:44:14,260
It still stands as a technological marvel.

695
00:44:14,360 --> 00:44:18,780
But in its simple, elegant, uplifting form,

696
00:44:18,880 --> 00:44:22,880
we have found a way to celebrate life and love.

697
00:45:29,020 --> 00:45:31,020
You

